Researcher: Bob Li
Research Institution: The Gwinnett School of Mathematics, Science and Technology
Architecture: Agent-based modeling, stochastic modeling
Key Findings and Personal Focuses
The MS2 bacteriophages eliminated the bacterial populations rapidly, reducing the ampicillin component to a secondary "clean-up" mechanism. This behavior demonstrates the E. coli population was kinetically overwhelmed and unable to release the F-plasmid in the alloted time.
Large Focuses on Randomness & Biological Fidelity: A significant portion of development was dedicated to optimizing the stochastic parameters and parameter spaces of the system from actual in-vitro studies, and through confirmed mathematical processes.

Mathematical & Statistical Frameworks
The simulation engine deployed quantitative architecture, utilizing:
a) Quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC) Simulation Pipelines
b) Latin Hypercube Sampling (LHS)
c) Inverse Normal Cumulative Distribution Function (norm.ppf) 
d) Factorial Analysis of Variance (Factorial ANOVA) 
e) Bliss Independence Synergy Matrix Modeling 
f) Non-Linear Hill Equations & Michaelis-Menten Pharmacodynamics
g) First-Order Kinetic Decay Rate Constants 

Software & Compute Stack
Vivarium Engine Core and integrated process/topology network modules
Anaconda Distribution via Jupyter Notebook environments 
Python 3 utilizing SciPy, Numpy, Matplotlib, and PANDAS

Limitations and Personal Opinions:
Thought was not given into bacteriophage resistance considering the easier evolutionary tradeoff of simply release F-plasmids, though it is true that the ampicillin selected for not the lost of F-plasmids but simply the evolution or change in the structure of previously existing F plasmids, though the pili likely would not have been lost
In vitro testing was limited due to regulations at the institute where research woudl have been performed, specifically regarding the usage of bacteriophages and available funding, as funding was set upon the researchers rather than the school.
